To root the TECNO SPARK 20 (Model: TECNO KJ5, Android 13, Helio G85) using an Ubuntu PC, you must first unlock the bootloader and then patch and flash the boot image. Because this device runs on a MediaTek Helio G85 processor, there are two distinct methods to accomplish this on Linux: the standard ADB & Fastboot method and the hardware-level MTKClient (BROM mode) method. [1] (https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zGyh_9OCnSo&vl=en), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AMc3KDIhZLY), [4] (https://droidwin.com/unlock-bootloader-tecno-spark/)
⚠️ Crucial Warning Before Starting:
• Data Loss: Unlocking the bootloader will completely wipe all data on your phone (factory reset). Back up everything first.
• Firmware Match: You must obtain the stock boot.img and vbmeta.img matching your exact build number (KJ5-H6931A...GHI-T-OP-241009V1507) to prevent bootloops.
 [1] (https://xdaforums.com/t/guide-rooting-tecno-spark-20-kj5n-twrp-custom-recovery.4667636/), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zGyh_9OCnSo&vl=en)

Phase 1: Preparation (Required for Both Methods)

1. Enable Developer Options on the Phone:
	• Go to Settings > My Phone.
	• Scroll down to Build number and tap it 7 times until you see "You are now a developer".
	• Go back to Settings > System > Developer options.
	• Toggle ON OEM unlocking and USB debugging. (Note: On newer Tecno firmware, you may also need to sign into a Tecno ID in Developer Options and wait up to 14 days for OEM unlocking permission). [1] (https://xdaforums.com/t/guide-rooting-tecno-spark-20-kj5n-twrp-custom-recovery.4667636/page-3), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zGyh_9OCnSo&vl=en), [3] (https://www.reddit.com/r/androidroot/comments/1wuwx4m/can_i_use_mtk_client_to_unlock_bootloader_on/)
2. Install Magisk on the Phone:
	• Download the latest official Magisk APK and install it on your TECNO SPARK 20. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gTd3CUT-6EE), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W7zUja0tzeA&t=516)
3. Prepare Your Stock Firmware Files:
	• Download the exact stock firmware ROM matching build KJ5-H6931A...GHI-T-OP-241009V1507.
	• Extract boot.img and vbmeta.img from the firmware package onto your Ubuntu machine. (If you don't have the firmware downloaded, Method 2 below lets you extract boot.img directly from your phone). [1] (https://xdaforums.com/t/guide-rooting-tecno-spark-20-kj5n-twrp-custom-recovery.4667636/)

Method 1: The Standard ADB & Fastboot Way (Recommended)

This is the safest and cleanest route if your phone allows standard fastboot unlocking. [1] (https://droidwin.com/unlock-bootloader-tecno-spark/)

Step 1: Set Up ADB & Fastboot on Ubuntu

Open your Ubuntu terminal (Ctrl + Alt + T) and install the Android platform tools: [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YLLQyiRvBkg), [2] (https://linuxhint.com/install-use-adb-fastboot-ubuntu/)
bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install android-tools-adb android-tools-fastboot -y
Use code with caution.

Step 2: Patch the Boot Image

1. Copy the stock boot.img to your phone's internal storage (e.g., Downloads folder).
2. Open the Magisk app on your phone.
3. Tap Install (next to Magisk) > Select and Patch a File, then choose the boot.img.
4. Once finished, copy the newly created magisk_patched-xxxxx.img back to your Ubuntu computer. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gTd3CUT-6EE), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W7zUja0tzeA&t=516)

Step 3: Unlock the Bootloader

1. Connect your phone to your Ubuntu PC via USB cable and accept the "Allow USB debugging" prompt on your phone screen.
2. In the Ubuntu terminal, verify the connection and reboot to fastboot:bash
adb devices
adb reboot bootloader
Use code with caution.
3. Once the phone enters Fastboot mode, verify fastboot sees it:bash
fastboot devices
Use code with caution.
4. Unlock the bootloader:bash
fastboot flashing unlock
Use code with caution.
5. Look at your phone screen—press the Volume Up button to confirm unlocking. Your phone will wipe its data and reboot.
6. Complete the initial Android setup again, re-enable USB debugging in Developer Options, and reboot back into fastboot (adb reboot bootloader). [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zGyh_9OCnSo&vl=en), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W7zUja0tzeA&t=516), [3] (https://droidwin.com/unlock-bootloader-tecno-spark/)

Step 4: Disable Verification & Flash Root

Navigate to the folder on Ubuntu containing your vbmeta.img and magisk_patched.img, then run:
bash
# Disable dm-verity so the phone boots with modified images
fastboot --disable-verity --disable-verification flash vbmeta vbmeta.img

# Flash the Magisk-patched boot image
fastboot flash boot magisk_patched-xxxxx.img

# Reboot the device
fastboot reboot
Use code with caution.
Once booted, open the Magisk app—it may ask to complete a quick environment setup and reboot once more. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W7zUja0tzeA&t=516)

Method 2: The Hardware BROM Way via MTKClient (No Firmware Download Needed)

Because your phone uses a MediaTek Helio G85, you can use MTKClient on Ubuntu to unlock the bootloader, dump your own boot.img directly from the phone, and flash it back without needing to hunt down a firmware ZIP online. (Note: If Tecno patched BROM access in the October 2024 security update 241009V1507, this method may fail to connect, requiring Method 1). [1] (https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient)

Step 1: Install MTKClient on Ubuntu

Run the following commands in your Ubuntu terminal:
bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3 git libusb-1.0-0 python3-pip -y
git clone https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient
cd mtkclient
pip3 install -r requirements.txt
sudo usermod -a -G plugdev $USER
sudo cp Setup/Linux/*.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
sudo udevadm control -R
Use code with caution.
(Log out and log back into Ubuntu if USB permissions fail).

Step 2: Unlock Bootloader & Dump Stock boot.img

1. Power off your TECNO SPARK 20 completely. [1] (https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient)
2. Run the unlock command in your terminal:bash
python3 mtk da seccfg unlock
Use code with caution.
3. Hold Volume Up + Volume Down simultaneously on the powered-off phone and plug in the USB cable. Release the buttons as soon as the terminal detects the device. [1] (https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient)
4. Once unlocked, disconnect the cable, power off the phone if it started booting, and dump your exact boot and vbmeta partitions:bash
python3 mtk r boot,vbmeta boot.img,vbmeta.img
Use code with caution.
(Hold Volume Up + Volume Down and plug in the USB cable again when prompted). [1] (https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient)

Step 3: Patch & Flash via MTKClient

1. Boot the phone up normally (it will factory reset from the unlock).
2. Copy the dumped boot.img to your phone, install the Magisk APK, and patch boot.img inside Magisk.
3. Copy the resulting magisk_patched.img back into your mtkclient folder on Ubuntu.
4. Power off your phone completely.
5. Flash the patched boot image and blank vbmeta using MTKClient:bash
python3 mtk w boot magisk_patched.img
Use code with caution.
6. Hold Volume Up + Volume Down and plug in the USB cable. Once the write finishes, unplug the cable and hold the Power button to boot into your rooted system. [1] (https://github.com/bkerler/mtkclient), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W7zUja0tzeA&t=516), [3] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gTd3CUT-6EE)

Optional Alternative: KernelSU (Instead of Magisk)

Some users on the r/androidroot subreddit note that the TECNO SPARK 20 can also be rooted using KernelSU if you prefer kernel-level root hiding over Magisk. The steps on Ubuntu are identical to Method 1 or 2, except you patch your boot.img using the KernelSU Manager app instead of Magisk before flashing. [1] (https://www.reddit.com/r/androidroot/comments/1n1zsi0/i_want_to_root_my_tecno_spark_20_kn5_but_its_my/)
